ML_PAGE("l10-prep", "<header class=\"page-header theme-l10\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">CHAPTER 10 · MCQ PREPARATION</span><h2>Chapter 10 MCQ Prep</h2><p>The complete MCQ preparation notes in their original order: 30 multiple-choice questions sit inside the sections they belong to, with the answer, the logic and the exam trap hidden until you reveal them.</p></header><div class=\"study-grid three-col\"><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>30</strong><span>MCQs in the notes</span></div><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>30</strong><span>Exam traps explained</span></div><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>30</strong><span>Difficult-mode questions</span></div></div><div class=\"action-row\"><button class=\"primary-btn bg-l10\" data-quiz=\"10\">Start Chapter 10 simulator</button></div><details class=\"topic-index toc-panel theme-l10\" id=\"l10p-toc\"><summary><strong>MCQ notes</strong><span class=\"toc-count\">2 sections</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><input type=\"search\" class=\"toc-search\" placeholder=\"Filter sections...\" aria-label=\"Filter sections\"><ul class=\"toc-list\"><li class=\"toc-group\">PART A - PRACTICE QUESTIONS</li><li data-full=\"Questions\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l10p-s2\" title=\"Questions\">Questions 1-30</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">PART B - FORMULAS AND ERROR DIAGNOSIS</li><li data-full=\"Formula map and error diagnosis\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l10p-s4\" title=\"Formula map and error diagnosis\">Formula map and error diagnosis</button></li></ul></details><details class=\"prep-notes theme-l10\" open><summary><div><strong>Chapter 10 MCQ preparation</strong><small>Exam strategy, practice questions, formula sheets and review - section by section, answers hidden.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"markdown-body compact\"><div class=\"notes-toolbar\"><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"open\">Expand all</button><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"close\">Collapse all</button><span>2 sections · click a heading to open it</span></div><h2>Chapter 10 - Fixed-Income Securities: MCQ Practice</h2>\n<p><strong>30 questions · suggested time: 45 minutes · practice target: 80%.</strong> These are illustrative study targets, not confirmed exam requirements. Choose one best answer and attempt the questions before reading the separate key. Unless stated otherwise, assume annual payments and ignore taxes and transaction costs.</p>\n<p>Reason through <strong>contractual cash flows → issuer and bond terms → market price and risk → investor needs</strong>. A promised payment is not a guaranteed outcome.</p><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l10p-d1\">PART A - PRACTICE QUESTIONS</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l10p-s2\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Questions</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q1</span><div><p>Which statement best describes an ordinary corporate bond?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It gives the holder residual ownership identical to common stock.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It is a debt claim with promised payments subject to its contractual terms and issuer credit risk.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It guarantees that the market price never changes.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It is an option that necessarily converts into equity.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>A corporate bond is a contractual debt claim; the issuer may fail to make promised payments.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A bondholder is generally a creditor, not a residual owner.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q2</span><div><p>A bond has RM1,000 par value and a 6% annual coupon rate. What is its annual coupon payment?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM6</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM60</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM600</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RM1,060</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Annual coupon = RM1,000 x 6% = <strong>RM60</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The coupon rate is applied to par, not the current trading price.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q3</span><div><p>The bond in Q2 sells for RM800. What is its current yield?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 6.0%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 12.5%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 8.0%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 7.5%</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Current yield = annual coupon / current price = RM60 / RM800 = <strong>7.5%</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Current yield omits the maturity gain or loss and is not yield to maturity.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q4</span><div><p>For a standard fixed-rate bond, the required market yield increases while promised cash flows remain unchanged. What generally happens to its price?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Price remains at par regardless of market rates.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Price rises because coupon payments increase automatically.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Price falls because its fixed future cash flows are discounted more heavily.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The coupon rate necessarily becomes the new market yield.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>A higher required yield reduces the present value of fixed promised cash flows.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Fixed-rate coupon amounts do not reset whenever the market yield changes.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q5</span><div><p>A conventional bond's coupon rate is 8%, while the market-required yield for its cash flows and risk is 6%. Under standard assumptions its price is generally:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Below par.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Above par.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Exactly zero.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Always equal to its annual coupon.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>A coupon above the required yield makes the promised cash flows attractive enough to trade at a premium under standard assumptions.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Premium means price above par; it does not change the contractual coupon.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q6</span><div><p>Two otherwise comparable noncallable fixed-rate bonds have identical coupon rates and yields but different maturities. Which is generally more sensitive in percentage-price terms to a small change in yield?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The longer-maturity bond.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The shorter-maturity bond in every case.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Neither; maturity has no role in interest-rate sensitivity.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Both must have exactly the same duration.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>For otherwise comparable fixed-coupon bonds, longer maturity generally gives greater yield sensitivity; duration captures the timing more directly.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> This is a conditional comparison, not a universal ranking of all bonds.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q7</span><div><p>An investor holds a bond promising RM60 annually. Inflation unexpectedly rises while the coupon stays fixed. Which risk does this most directly illustrate?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Stock split risk.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Shareholder voting risk.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Purchasing-power risk.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A guaranteed real gain.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Unexpected inflation can erode the purchasing power of fixed nominal payments.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Receiving all promised ringgit does not guarantee preserved real purchasing power.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q8</span><div><p>A bondholder cannot sell quickly without accepting a large price concession. This is primarily:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Guaranteed default.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A stock dividend.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Liquidity risk.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A coupon reset.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Liquidity risk concerns the difficulty or cost of selling promptly at a reasonable price.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Illiquidity is distinct from issuer default, though both can occur.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q9</span><div><p>A company calls a bond before maturity after market interest rates fall. What risk is most relevant to its investor?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The bond must become common stock.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The investor is guaranteed all remaining original coupons to maturity.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The investor may have to reinvest the proceeds at a lower rate.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Its market value was immune to interest rates before the call.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>A call can end attractive coupons early and expose the investor to reinvestment at lower rates.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Callable debt does not promise every original coupon until scheduled maturity.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q10</span><div><p>A bond has RM1,000 par and a call premium of RM40. What is the call price under these stated terms?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM40</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM960</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM1,000</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RM1,040</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Call price = RM1,000 + RM40 = <strong>RM1,040</strong> under the given terms.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The premium is added to par rather than subtracted.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q11</span><div><p>A deferred-call provision generally means:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The investor can never sell the bond.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The issuer cannot exercise the stated call option until a specified protection period has passed.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Interest will always be deferred forever.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The bond has no maturity date.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Deferred call protection delays the issuer's ability to call according to the contract.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Deferred call does not prevent the holder from trading the bond.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q12</span><div><p>A bond covenant prohibits early repayment using proceeds from a lower-coupon replacement issue for a specified period. This is best described as:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> An increase in coupon rate.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A conversion ratio.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Refunding protection.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A shareholders' voting right.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>A refunding restriction limits early retirement financed by lower-rate replacement debt during its protection period.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Refunding restrictions and general call protection are related but their actual terms can differ.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q13</span><div><p>A sinking-fund provision requires an issuer to retire a portion of a bond issue over time. What is a plausible investor consequence?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It guarantees the issuer cannot default.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Debt repayment is scheduled, but an individual bond may be retired before final maturity under the issue's terms.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It makes the entire issue common equity.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It guarantees that every investor receives exactly the market price at every date.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>A sinking fund sets aside resources or retires portions of an issue; specific holders can face early repayment according to the indenture.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A repayment schedule does not eliminate default or reinvestment risk.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q14</span><div><p>A bond secured by a claim on identified real estate is most accurately described as:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A mortgage bond.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> An unsecured debenture.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Common stock.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A depositary receipt.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>A mortgage bond is secured by specified real estate.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A debenture is normally unsecured; collateral does not guarantee full recovery.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q15</span><div><p>A subordinated debenture generally differs from a senior secured bond because the debenture:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Automatically owns the issuer's real estate.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Has a guaranteed government payment.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Always has a shorter maturity.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Is unsecured and has a lower-priority claim than specified senior creditors.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>A subordinated debenture is unsecured and has lower priority than the senior claims specified in its terms.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Higher priority affects recovery, not a guarantee of repayment.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q16</span><div><p>An issue receives an investment-grade credit rating. Which conclusion is most defensible?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The market price cannot fall when yields rise.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The rating is an opinion of relative creditworthiness, not a guarantee against default or price loss.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The bond has no liquidity risk.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> All agencies must agree and never change their assessments.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>A credit rating is an agency's opinion about credit risk and can change; it does not eliminate market risk.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Investment grade is not risk-free.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q17</span><div><p>Which statement best contrasts general obligation and revenue municipal bonds in the course's basic framework?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Revenue bonds are common stocks, while general obligations are options.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> General obligation bonds rely on the issuer's general credit; revenue bonds rely on specified project or enterprise revenues, subject to actual terms.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Both are necessarily free of default risk.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Revenue bonds guarantee that project income exceeds debt service.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>These describe the common pledged-payment distinctions, which must be checked against actual bond terms.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Neither municipal label is a blanket guarantee.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q18</span><div><p>An investor can earn a hypothetical tax-exempt 4% bond yield. If an otherwise comparable taxable bond's interest is taxed at a flat 20% and no other taxes apply, what taxable yield gives the same after-tax yield?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 4%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 3.2%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 5%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 20%</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Taxable-equivalent yield = 4% / (1 - 0.20) = <strong>5%</strong>. Check: 5%(1 - 0.20) = 4%.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The tax rate and exemption are hypothetical; actual tax treatment depends on jurisdiction and circumstances.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q19</span><div><p>Which description of inflation-indexed government securities is most accurate?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Their contractual adjustment can help protect against specified inflation, but their market prices can still fluctuate.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> They guarantee that their market price can never fall.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> They eliminate all taxes, liquidity risk and inflation measurement differences.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> They are identical to a fixed-price bank deposit in every respect.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Contractual inflation adjustments can protect against the referenced inflation measure, while real yields and market prices still move.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Inflation protection does not remove every investment risk.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q20</span><div><p>A bond pays no periodic coupon and promises RM1,000 at maturity. What is it commonly called?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> An income share.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A zero-coupon bond.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A floating dividend.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A common-stock split.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>A zero-coupon bond pays no periodic coupon and is normally issued or traded at a discount to its maturity value.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> No periodic interest does not mean zero total return.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q21</span><div><p>What is a key risk for an investor in a mortgage-backed security when borrowers repay mortgages earlier than expected?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The security necessarily has no credit or interest-rate exposure.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Earlier repayment always guarantees a higher realized yield.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A mortgage pool cannot repay principal before final maturity.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Prepayment may return principal sooner and force reinvestment at then-current rates.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Mortgage prepayments can change payment timing and create reinvestment risk, especially when rates fall.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Original scheduled cash-flow timing is not assured.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q22</span><div><p>A collateralized mortgage security is divided into tranches. Why does the division matter?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Tranches remove all uncertainty from mortgage cash flows.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Every tranche necessarily receives identical payments and risks.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Different tranches may receive cash flows in different sequences and bear different timing or credit risks.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The securities become common shares of every borrower.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Tranche rules allocate principal and other risks differently across classes.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Pooling and slicing cash flows do not eliminate risk.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q23</span><div><p>A high-yield bond usually has which characteristic relative to an otherwise comparable investment-grade bond?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A lower credit rating and generally higher compensation for credit risk, without a guaranteed higher realized return.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A government guarantee of repayment.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> No possibility of default.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Guaranteed positive returns if held for one year.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>High-yield debt is below investment grade and generally offers higher yield in compensation for greater credit risk.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Higher promised yield need not become a higher realized return.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q24</span><div><p>A Malaysian investor buys a USD-denominated foreign bond. Which statement about currency risk is correct?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The investor's RM return can change as the USD/RM exchange rate changes.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Denomination in USD eliminates currency risk for every investor worldwide.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The exchange rate matters only if the issuer is Malaysian.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Currency risk makes credit and interest-rate risks disappear.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>A USD payment must be converted to RM for this investor; the RM value changes with USD/RM rates.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The slides' no-currency-risk claim for USD-denominated bonds assumes a USD-based investor.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q25</span><div><p>What does a convertible bond's conversion ratio specify?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The number of years until a stock split.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The bond's annual coupon divided by its market price.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The issuer's income tax rate.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The number of common shares received upon conversion under the contract.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>The conversion ratio is the number of shares obtainable per bond, subject to the conversion terms.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> It is distinct from conversion price and current yield.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q26</span><div><p>A convertible has RM1,000 par and a conversion price of RM40 per share. Under the stated convention, what is its conversion ratio?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 25 shares per bond.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 40 shares per bond.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 1,000 shares per bond.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 40,000 shares per bond.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Conversion ratio = RM1,000 par / RM40 conversion price = <strong>25 shares per bond</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Use contractual par and conversion price, not market bond price.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q27</span><div><p>A convertible bond can be exchanged for 25 shares, each worth RM36. What is its conversion value?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM900.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM625.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM1,000.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RM1,111.11.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Conversion value = 25 shares x RM36 = <strong>RM900</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Conversion value is the shares' market value, not necessarily the convertible's own trading price.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q28</span><div><p>A convertible bond trades for RM1,050 and is convertible into 25 shares. What is the conversion equivalent price per share?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM1,025.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM25.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM40.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RM42.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Conversion equivalent price = RM1,050 / 25 = <strong>RM42 per share</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> This uses convertible market price, unlike contractual conversion price.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q29</span><div><p>A convertible's market price is RM1,050 and its conversion value is RM900. Under the course convention, what are its conversion premium and premium as a percentage of conversion value?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM1,050 and 116.67%.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM150 and 14.29%.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM900 and 85.71%.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RM150 and approximately 16.67%.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Premium = RM1,050 - RM900 = <strong>RM150</strong>; percentage = RM150 / RM900 x 100 = <strong>16.67%</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Follow the stated convention: percentage denominator is conversion value, not convertible market price.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q30</span><div><p>A convertible has a RM150 conversion premium and pays RM70 annual interest; the shares obtainable by conversion pay RM40 in total annual dividends. Ignoring taxes, changes and time value, what is the simple premium payback period?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 5 years.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 2.14 years.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 3.75 years.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The premium is recovered immediately.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Incremental annual income = RM70 - RM40 = RM30. Simple payback = RM150 / RM30 = <strong>5 years</strong>, ignoring time value and changes. If incremental income is zero or negative, the premium is not recovered through this method.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Use the difference in annual income, not the full bond coupon.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l10p-d3\">PART B - FORMULAS AND ERROR DIAGNOSIS</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l10p-s4\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Formula map and error diagnosis</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"l10_conv_map\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Five convertible measures from three inputs. Each arrow is one division, multiplication or subtraction.</figcaption></figure></div><ul>\n<li>Annual coupon = par x coupon rate; current yield = annual coupon / market price. Coupon rate, current yield and yield to maturity measure different things.</li>\n<li>Under a single hypothetical tax rate, taxable-equivalent yield = tax-exempt yield / (1 - tax rate). Apply real tax law only after checking relevant circumstances.</li>\n<li>Conversion ratio = par / contractual conversion price; conversion value = conversion ratio x share price; conversion equivalent = convertible market price / conversion ratio.</li>\n<li>Conversion premium = convertible market price - conversion value; premium percentage = premium / conversion value. Simple payback = premium / (annual bond interest - annual dividends on the conversion shares), when the income difference is positive.</li>\n<li>Distinguish default, interest-rate, purchasing-power, liquidity, prepayment, call and currency risks. Check the investor's home currency, bond terms, denominator and payment timeline before deciding.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>Source note:</em> Original questions based on Chapter 10, <em>Fixed-Income Securities</em> (Pearson slides, 2014). Municipal-tax and currency examples use expressly stated assumptions, not current legal or tax advice. Agency guarantees depend on the issuer and issue; a government-related label alone does not make every security Treasury-guaranteed. Study targets are illustrative.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"question-bank-wrap hard-wrap\" id=\"l10-hard\"><summary><div><strong>Difficult mode - 30 questions</strong><small>18 Bloom-graded questions (3 per level) and 12 marked extension questions. Answers hidden.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><p class=\"hard-intro\">Questions climb Bloom&#39;s taxonomy from <b>Remember</b> to <b>Create</b>. The <b>extension</b> questions go beyond the slides (industry practice, CFA-style, case competitions, Master&#39;s, PhD and postdoc level) but can be reasoned out from the chapter&#39;s principles.</p><div class=\"action-row\"><button class=\"primary-btn\" data-quiz=\"10\" data-quiz-mode=\"difficult\">Start Chapter 10 difficult mode</button><button class=\"secondary-btn\" data-quiz=\"10\" data-quiz-mode=\"extensions\">Extensions only</button></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D1</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></div><p>Which pairing of a bond term with its meaning is correct?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Serial bond: the entire issue matures on a single date, so every bond in it is repaid on the same day.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Refunding provision: the bondholder's right to sell the bond back to the issuer.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Deferred call: the issuer may not call the bond until a stated protection period has passed.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Debenture: a bond secured by specified pieces of equipment.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>A deferred call gives call protection for an initial period. A describes a term bond (a serial bond matures in portions on different dates). B describes a put; a refunding provision prohibits early retirement financed by a lower-coupon refunding issue. D describes an equipment trust certificate; a debenture is unsecured.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D2</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></div><p>Which bond is backed by stocks or bonds held in trust by a third party?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A collateral trust bond</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> An equipment trust certificate</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A mortgage bond</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> An income bond</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Collateral trust bonds pledge securities held in trust. Equipment trust certificates pledge equipment such as railcars or aircraft, mortgage bonds pledge real estate, and an income bond is distinguished by interest that depends on earnings, not by its collateral.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D3</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></div><p>In the slides, which description defines the <strong>conversion equivalent</strong> of a convertible bond?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Par value divided by the conversion ratio.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The conversion ratio multiplied by the current market price of the issuer's common stock.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The convertible bond's current market price minus the value of the shares it converts into.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The share price at which conversion would be worth exactly the convertible's market price.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Conversion equivalent $= P_c / N$: the price the stock would have to reach to justify today's bond price. A is the contractual conversion price, B is conversion value and C is the conversion premium in ringgit.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D4</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></div><p>Market yields fall sharply. Why does a callable bond's price usually rise <strong>less</strong> than the price of an otherwise identical noncallable bond?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Callable bonds are legally required to pay lower coupons, so their prices respond less to any change in market yields.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Buyers will not pay much above the call price for a bond the issuer is now likely to redeem at that price.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The call premium is subtracted from par once yields fall.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Falling yields increase the issuer's default risk, which lowers the price.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Once refinancing is cheaper, a call becomes likely, and nobody pays far above the call price for a bond that will be taken back at it: the call caps price appreciation. A is not a rule, C reverses the call price (par plus premium), and falling yields do not by themselves raise default risk.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D5</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></div><p>A conventional fixed-rate bond's <strong>current yield is above its coupon rate</strong>. What must be true?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Its yield to maturity equals its coupon rate.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The bond must be callable.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The issuer has cut the coupon payment.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The bond is trading below par.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Current yield $= C / P$ and coupon rate $= C / \\text{par}$ share the same numerator, so current yield exceeds the coupon rate only when $P$ is below par. Nothing about the coupon has changed (C), a discount bond's YTM is above its coupon rate (A), and callability is irrelevant (B).</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D6</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></div><p>A slide says dollar-denominated (Yankee) bonds remove currency risk. Why is a ringgit-based investor who buys one still exposed to currency risk?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The claim assumes a dollar-based investor; a ringgit investor must still convert USD payments into RM.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Yankee bonds actually pay their coupons in euros, so a second conversion is needed.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Currency risk can only arise from the issuer's home country, which is foreign.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Registration with the SEC requires each payment to be converted into the buyer's home currency at a fixed contractual rate.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Currency risk depends on the investor's home currency, not the bond's label. For a USD-based investor a USD bond needs no conversion; for a ringgit investor it does. B and D are false, and C confuses issuer country risk with currency risk.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D7</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></div><p>A $\\text{RM}1{,}000$ par bond with a $7.5\\%$ coupon <strong>paid semiannually</strong> trades at $\\text{RM}1{,}060$. What are the semiannual coupon payment and the current yield?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> $\\text{RM}37.50$ and $3.54\\%$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $\\text{RM}75.00$ and $7.50\\%$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> $\\text{RM}37.50$ and $7.08\\%$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> $\\text{RM}75.00$ and $7.08\\%$</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Annual coupon $= 7.5\\% \\times 1{,}000 = \\text{RM}75$, paid as $\\text{RM}37.50$ every six months. Current yield uses the annual coupon: $75 / 1{,}060 = \\mathbf{7.08\\%}$. A divides the half-year coupon by the price, B ignores the premium price, and D gives the annual amount, not the semiannual payment.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D8</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></div><p>A municipal bond yields $3.6\\%$ and is exempt from both federal and state tax. Using the slide's combined formula, what is its taxable-equivalent yield for an investor with a $30\\%$ federal and a $6\\%$ state tax rate?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> $5.14\\%$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $5.47\\%$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> $5.63\\%$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> $2.37\\%$</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Combined rate $= 0.30 + 0.06(1 - 0.30) = 0.342$, so $TEY = 3.6\\% / (1 - 0.342) = 3.6\\% / 0.658 = \\mathbf{5.47\\%}$. A uses the federal rate only, C simply adds the rates ($3.6\\% / 0.64$), and D multiplies by $(1 - t)$ instead of dividing.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D9</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></div><p>A convertible has $\\text{RM}1{,}000$ par and a conversion price of $\\text{RM}62.50$. The stock trades at $\\text{RM}55$ and the bond at $\\text{RM}960$. What is the conversion premium as a percentage of conversion value?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> $9.09\\%$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $8.33\\%$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> $13.64\\%$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> $4.17\\%$</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Ratio $= 1{,}000 / 62.50 = 16$; conversion value $= 16 \\times 55 = \\text{RM}880$; premium $= 960 - 880 = \\text{RM}80$; percentage $= 80 / 880 = \\mathbf{9.09\\%}$. B divides by the bond price, C compares the conversion price with the share price ($7.50 / 55$), and D halves the premium for no reason.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D10</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></div><p>Two default-free bonds both yield $5\\%$: Bond X is a two-year $5\\%$ annual-coupon bond and Bond Y is a $25$-year zero-coupon bond. Required yields on both rise to $6\\%$. Which statement is correct?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Both prices fall by about the same percentage, because both bonds are default-free and yield the same $5\\%$ at the start.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> X falls more than Y because only X pays coupons that are now below market.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Neither price changes, because government bonds are repaid at par.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Y falls by roughly $21\\%$ and X by roughly $2\\%$, because Y's only payment is $25$ years away.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>X: $\\text{RM}1{,}000 \\to 50 / 1.06 + 1{,}050 / 1.06^{2} = \\text{RM}981.67$, about $-1.83\\%$. Y: $1{,}000 / 1.05^{25} = \\text{RM}295.30 \\to 1{,}000 / 1.06^{25} = \\text{RM}233.00$, about $-21.1\\%$. Default-free does not mean free of interest-rate risk (A, C), and the long zero is far more sensitive than the short coupon bond (B).</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D11</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></div><p>A defaulted company has $\\text{RM}150\\text{m}$ left after costs. Secured debt of $\\text{RM}100\\text{m}$ is backed by collateral worth $\\text{RM}60\\text{m}$; senior unsecured debt is $\\text{RM}120\\text{m}$; subordinated debt is $\\text{RM}50\\text{m}$. The secured shortfall ranks equally with senior unsecured debt. Under strict priority, what fraction of its claim does the <strong>senior unsecured</strong> debt recover?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> $75\\%$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $100\\%$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> $56.25\\%$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> $41.67\\%$</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Collateral pays the secured holders $\\text{RM}60\\text{m}$, leaving $\\text{RM}90\\text{m}$. Their $\\text{RM}40\\text{m}$ shortfall joins the $\\text{RM}120\\text{m}$ senior unsecured claims: $90 / 160 = \\mathbf{56.25\\%}$ each. A ignores the shortfall claim ($90 / 120$); D pays the secured debt in full first ($50 / 120$), which wrongly treats security as a guarantee of full recovery.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D12</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></div><p>A sequential-pay CMO has tranches A (short), B and C. Rates rise sharply and mortgage prepayments slow. What is the most direct effect?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Tranche A is retired sooner than expected, and tranche B starts receiving principal earlier than originally scheduled.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Principal arrives more slowly: A takes longer to retire, and B and C wait longer for principal (extension risk).</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Tranche C now receives principal before tranche A.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Interest payments stop for every tranche until rates fall again.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Slower prepayments stretch the principal stream; under the sequential rule A is retired later, delaying B and C. A describes the prepayment case (rates falling), C contradicts the waterfall, and D confuses principal timing with interest.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D13</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></div><p>A retiree in the $15\\%$ tax bracket compares a tax-exempt municipal bond yielding $3.4\\%$ with a taxable corporate bond yielding $4.2\\%$; rating, maturity, liquidity and call terms appear similar. Which conclusion is best supported?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The taxable bond: its after-tax yield of $3.57\\%$ beats $3.40\\%$, provided the risks and tax treatment really are comparable.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The municipal bond, because tax-exempt bonds are always the better choice for retirees.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The municipal bond, because its taxable-equivalent yield of $4.00\\%$ is above its own $3.40\\%$ yield, so the exemption adds value.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Either bond, because the $0.8$-point yield gap is exactly offset by the $15\\%$ tax.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>$4.2\\% \\times 0.85 = 3.57\\% &gt; 3.40\\%$; equivalently $TEY = 3.4\\% / 0.85 = 4.00\\% &lt; 4.2\\%$. At a low bracket the exemption is worth little. C compares the TEY with the wrong yield, B ignores the bracket, and D is arithmetic that does not hold.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D14</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></div><p>An investor says: \"My convertible's bond floor is $\\text{RM}900$, so I cannot lose more than $\\text{RM}200$ on the $\\text{RM}1{,}100$ I paid.\" Which evaluation is best?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Correct: the bond floor is a contractual minimum price written into the indenture and enforced by the trustee for every holder.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Correct, but only if the bond is callable.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Incorrect: the floor is only a model estimate of straight-bond value, and it falls if credit weakens or yields rise.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Incorrect, because the conversion value is the guaranteed minimum instead.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The bond floor is the value of the debt component under current yields and credit, not a promise. If the company struggles, the shares fall and the floor falls with its credit at the same time. Conversion value is not guaranteed either (D), and callability is irrelevant (B).</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D15</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></div><p>An investor must pay a house deposit in exactly three years and cannot afford a shortfall. Which holding best fits the need?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A $30$-year AAA government bond, because its top rating removes the price risk of selling it in year $3$ and it yields more.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A three-year BB corporate bond, because its higher yield compensates for any default.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A mortgage-backed security with an expected three-year life, because asset backing makes it safe.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A three-year government zero-coupon bond: one payment on the date needed, very low default risk, nothing to reinvest.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Matching the maturity to the liability removes price and reinvestment uncertainty about the nominal amount. A must be sold at an unknown price, B adds meaningful default risk, and C has uncertain timing (prepayment and extension) as well as credit risk.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D16</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></div><p>You are writing monitoring rules for a client's portfolio of <strong>callable investment-grade corporate bonds</strong> held for income. Which set of reassessment triggers is most complete?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Re-check a bond only after it misses a coupon payment.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Reassess on a downgrade or wider spread, on rate falls that make calls likely near call dates, and on changes in the client's needs.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Reassess only when the stock market falls by more than $20\\%$, since bond and stock risks are driven by the same events.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Sell every bond whenever market interest rates rise, because rising rates always signal that issuers are about to default.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The triggers map to the risks that matter here: credit (downgrades, spreads), call and reinvestment risk (falling rates near call dates) and suitability (the client's needs). A reacts too late, C watches the wrong market, and D sells for interest-rate moves that a hold-to-maturity income investor may not need to act on.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D17</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></div><p>An adviser needs one rule for choosing between a tax-exempt bond yielding $3.0\\%$ and an otherwise identical taxable bond yielding $4.5\\%$ for clients in different brackets. Which rule is correct?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Choose the tax-exempt bond for any client who pays income tax at all.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Choose whichever bond has the higher pre-tax yield.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Choose the tax-exempt bond when the client's marginal rate exceeds $1 - 3.0\\% / 4.5\\% = 33.3\\%$; otherwise choose the taxable bond.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Choose the tax-exempt bond when the client's marginal rate exceeds $3.0\\% / 4.5\\% = 66.7\\%$, the ratio of the two yields; otherwise the taxable one.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The bonds tie when $4.5\\%(1 - t) = 3.0\\%$, so $t^{*} = 1 - 3.0 / 4.5 = 33.3\\%$. Above it the exemption is worth more. A and B ignore the bracket; D inverts the ratio.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D18</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></div><p>An investor will only buy a convertible if its simple payback is at most $4$ years. The bond trades at $\\text{RM}1{,}090$, converts into $25$ shares priced at $\\text{RM}40$, and each share pays $\\text{RM}0.80$ a year. What is the <strong>minimum</strong> annual coupon the bond must pay?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> $\\text{RM}42.50$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $\\text{RM}22.50$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> $\\text{RM}20.00$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> $\\text{RM}272.50$</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Conversion value $= 25 \\times 40 = \\text{RM}1{,}000$, so the premium is $\\text{RM}90$. Payback $\\le 4$ needs extra income $\\ge 90 / 4 = \\text{RM}22.50$ on top of the dividends of $25 \\times 0.80 = \\text{RM}20$: coupon $\\ge \\mathbf{\\text{RM}42.50}$. B forgets the dividends, C is the dividends alone, and D divides the whole bond price by $4$.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension questions</span><small>beyond the notes, lowest Bloom level first</small></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X9</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · PhD</span></div><p>Why are mortgage-backed securities said to have <strong>negative convexity</strong> when interest rates fall?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Falling rates speed up prepayments, so principal returns at par just when the price would rise, capping gains like a call.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Falling rates raise mortgage default rates sharply, which lowers the price of the security just when other bonds are rising.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Mortgage coupons reset upward when market rates fall.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Tranching converts the security's interest into principal.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Homeowners hold a prepayment (call) option. When rates fall they refinance, so the investor's price gains are limited by early return of principal. That is the same mechanism as call risk. B, C and D describe effects that are not the cause.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X1</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Industry</span></div><p>A corporate bond yields $6.35\\%$ and a government bond of the same maturity yields $4.90\\%$. A trader quotes the credit spread in basis points. What is it?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> $1.45$ bp</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $14.5$ bp</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> $1{,}450$ bp</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> $145$ bp</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>$6.35\\% - 4.90\\% = 1.45\\%$, and $1$ bp $= 0.01\\%$, so the spread is $1.45 / 0.01 = \\mathbf{145}$ bp. The other answers misplace the decimal.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X3</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · CFA</span></div><p>A $12$-year zero-coupon bond with $\\text{RM}1{,}000$ par is priced to yield $5.5\\%$ a year with annual compounding. What is its price?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> $\\text{RM}525.98$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $\\text{RM}602.41$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> $\\text{RM}947.87$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> $\\text{RM}521.48$</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>$P = 1{,}000 / 1.055^{12} = \\mathbf{\\text{RM}525.98}$. B uses simple interest ($1{,}000 / 1.66$), C discounts for one year only, and D uses semiannual compounding ($1{,}000 / 1.0275^{24}$), which the question does not specify.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X2</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Industry</span></div><p>A Malaysian insurer's USD bond returns $6\\%$ in dollars over a year, while the dollar falls $8\\%$ against the ringgit. What is the insurer's return in ringgit?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> $-2.00\\%$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $-2.48\\%$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> $+14.00\\%$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> $+2.48\\%$</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>$(1.06)(0.92) - 1 = 0.9752 - 1 = \\mathbf{-2.48\\%}$. A simply adds the two returns and misses the cross term $0.06 \\times (-0.08) = -0.48\\%$; C adds the currency fall as if it were a gain; D has the wrong sign.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X4</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · CFA</span></div><p>All four bonds yield $6\\%$ and have $\\text{RM}1{,}000$ par. Which has the largest <strong>percentage</strong> price fall if yields rise to $7\\%$?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A five-year $8\\%$ annual-coupon bond</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A five-year zero-coupon bond</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A $20$-year zero-coupon bond</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A $20$-year $10\\%$ annual-coupon bond</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Price changes: A about $-3.99\\%$, B about $-4.59\\%$, C about $-17.12\\%$, D about $-9.66\\%$. Longer maturity and no coupons both push more value into distant cash flows, so the long zero is the most sensitive; a high coupon (D) returns cash earlier and dampens the effect.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X7</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Master's</span></div><p>The issuer of a convertible is downgraded, but its share price is unchanged. All else equal, what happens?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The bond floor falls, so the convertible's price falls toward conversion value and the conversion premium shrinks.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Conversion value falls because the downgrade reduces the conversion ratio.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The conversion ratio rises to compensate bondholders automatically.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The conversion premium rises, because a weaker issuer makes the option to switch into its shares more valuable to holders.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>A downgrade raises the yield used to value the debt component, lowering the bond floor. Conversion value ($N P_s$) is unchanged because neither the ratio nor the share price moved (B, C), so the premium $P_c - CV$ narrows; nothing in the downgrade makes the option itself more valuable (D).</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X10</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · PhD</span></div><p>The slide's combined taxable-equivalent yield uses the total rate $t_f + t_s(1 - t_f)$ rather than $t_f + t_s$. Under which assumption is that expression exact, and what is the total rate for $t_f = 35\\%$ and $t_s = 8\\%$?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Federal tax is charged on the state tax only, so the two rates simply add up; $43.0\\%$, the federal plus the state rate.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> State tax is charged on federal tax; $37.8\\%$.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The municipal coupon is taxed twice; $43.0\\%$.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> State tax is deductible federally, so the total is $t_s + t_f(1 - t_s)$, the same thing; $40.2\\%$.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Tax on $\\text{RM}1$ of taxable interest: state $t_s$, then federal $t_f(1 - t_s)$ on the remainder; total $t_s + t_f - t_f t_s$, the same as $t_f + t_s(1 - t_f)$. With the numbers: $0.35 + 0.08 \\times 0.65 = 0.402 = \\mathbf{40.2\\%}$. A and C simply add the rates ($43\\%$), which double-counts; B is not the slide's model.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X5</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Case competition</span></div><p>A client in the $37\\%$ bracket chooses between a BBB toll-road revenue bond yielding $3.9\\%$ tax-exempt (thinly traded) and an AA taxable corporate bond yielding $5.8\\%$. Which recommendation is best?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The revenue bond automatically, because once the taxable-equivalent yield exceeds the corporate yield, rating and liquidity differences no longer matter.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Not on yield alone: the $6.19\\%$ TEY beats $5.80\\%$, but the lower rating, toll-revenue dependence and illiquidity must be weighed.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The corporate bond, because $5.80\\%$ is higher than $3.90\\%$.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The corporate bond, because its after-tax yield of $3.65\\%$ exceeds the revenue bond's $3.90\\%$.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>$TEY = 3.9\\% / 0.63 = 6.19\\%$ and the corporate's after-tax yield is $5.8\\% \\times 0.63 = 3.65\\% &lt; 3.90\\%$, so the revenue bond wins on yield (D's claim is false). But the TEY comparison assumes equal risk, and these bonds differ in rating, payment source and liquidity (A). C compares a pre-tax with an after-tax yield.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X8</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Master's</span></div><p>Convertible A has a $\\text{RM}150$ premium and $\\text{RM}50$ of extra annual income; convertible B has an $\\text{RM}80$ premium and $\\text{RM}20$ of extra income. An analyst picks A because its payback is shorter. What is the strongest critique?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The analyst miscalculated: A's payback is longer than B's.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Payback periods should be computed with the full coupon rather than the extra income, which would reverse the ranking.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Payback ignores time value, changing dividends and share prices, and the premium at risk: A costs almost twice as much premium.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A shorter payback always means a higher total return over the bond's life, so choosing A on this basis alone is correct.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>A pays back in $150 / 50 = 3$ years and B in $80 / 20 = 4$ years, so A is not miscalculated. Payback is a crude income measure: it ignores discounting, what happens after payback, and how much is at risk if the shares fall. B is wrong because payback divides by the EXTRA income over the dividends, not the full coupon, and D treats payback as a return measure.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X11</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Postdoc</span></div><p>A bond is downgraded from BBB- to BB+, and funds that may hold only investment-grade debt must sell it. Its yield jumps far more than the change in estimated default losses would justify. Which interpretation is best supported?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The jump proves that the agencies' new estimate of default risk is exactly right and that the market agrees with it fully.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Forced selling by rating-constrained funds adds a temporary price concession: part of the jump is liquidity, not default risk.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Ratings have no effect on prices because they are only opinions.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The bond's coupon must have been cut at the time of the downgrade, which is why the yield moved by so much more.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>A rating is an opinion, but rules tied to it change who can hold the bond. Selling pressure at the investment-grade boundary can push the price below what credit risk alone implies. A overstates what a price reveals, C ignores rating-linked constraints, and the coupon is unchanged by a downgrade (D).</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X6</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Case competition</span></div><p>A family must pay $\\text{RM}100{,}000$ in two years and another $\\text{RM}100{,}000$ in five years. Which bond portfolio best locks in both amounts?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> One $30$-year government bond, selling part of it in years $2$ and $5$.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A high-yield convertible bond whose conversion value is expected to grow.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A mortgage pass-through security with an expected average life of three and a half years, which averages out the two dates.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Two government zero-coupon bonds maturing in years $2$ and $5$, each with $\\text{RM}100{,}000$ face value.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Each zero delivers a known nominal amount on the liability date with very low default risk and no reinvestment of coupons. A forces sales at unknown prices, B adds credit and equity risk, and C has uncertain timing (prepayment and extension).</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X12</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Postdoc</span></div><p>You want to explain a convertible's market price with a model rather than with the slide's premium measures. Which decomposition is internally consistent?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Value = conversion value + par value, because the holder receives the shares and is also repaid the par value at maturity.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Value = conversion premium ÷ payback period, because income repays the premium.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Value = straight-bond value at the issuer's risky yield + value of the option to exchange the bond for $N$ shares.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Value = current yield x conversion ratio, because both measure income per share.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>A convertible is a straight bond plus an exchange option; the bond component gives the floor and the option gives the upside, which is why the price curve sits above both the floor and conversion value. A double-counts (the holder gets one or the other), B and D combine measures that have no valuation meaning.</p></div></details></div></details></div></div></details>");
